# bug-nilpy-input-builtin-is-shadowed-by-pascals-standard-input-file
#
# `input()` never reached a call lowering: `Input` is a STANDARD PASCAL
# IDENTIFIER (the text file), so `line = input()` bound the file variable
# instead. Nothing complained there — the failure surfaced later and
# unrecognisably at the first USE of the result, e.g. `len(line)` reporting
# "expected newline after statement" with a position pointing into
# builtinheap's own source.
#
# That is what stopped the uforth corpus at its `line = input()`, and it is why
# the name needs an explicit NilPy arm rather than the ordinary builtin route.
# Pascal's Input is untouched: the arm is gated on PyExprMode, and on there
# being no user proc of that name, so a NilPy `def input()` still wins.
#
# Driven from the Makefile with stdin piped in, like test_eof_stdin.pas.

a = input()
print("first:" + a)

b = input("prompt> ")
print("second:" + b)

print(len(a), len(b))
print(a.upper())

# the result is a real string: concatenation, slicing, comparison
print(a + "-" + b)
print(a[0], b[0])
print(a == "one", a == "two")
